<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mu of Balhae</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 719–737</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Dae Muye, King Mu of Balhae</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mu, personal name Dae Muye, was the second king of Balhae, reigning from 719 to 737, and son of its founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dae-joyeong">Dae Joyeong</a>. He expanded the kingdom over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohe">Mohe</a> tribes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and used his own reign title. His attack on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heishui-mohe">Heishui Mohe</a> led to war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, including a naval raid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dengzhou">Dengzhou</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> in 732, and he also opened relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 727.</p></div>
